3. Scale up
============

Infrastructure diagram (whiteboard description):

                              [User]
                                |
                    DNS: www.foobar.com -> Load Balancer cluster IP
                                |
                                v
                  [Load Balancer 1 (HAProxy)] <--cluster--> [Load Balancer 2 (HAProxy)]
                          (Active)                              (Passive/Backup)
                                |
                    -------------------------
                    |                       |
                    v                       v
             [Web server 1]          [Web server 2]
              (Nginx)                  (Nginx)
                    \                       /
                     \                     /
                      v                   v
                  [Application server 1]  [Application server 2]
                  - Codebase               - Codebase
                      \                       /
                       \                     /
                        v                   v
                   [Database server (Primary)]
                   [Database server (Replica)]

Explanation, starting from the user:

A user types www.foobar.com in their browser. DNS resolves this to the
IP address shared by the load balancer cluster. The request reaches
whichever load balancer (HAProxy) is currently active. That load
balancer then forwards the request to one of the web servers, which
forwards it to an application server, which in turn talks to the
database to read or write data, then the response travels back the
same path to the user.

Unlike the previous (distributed) infrastructure, where each server
contained the web server, application server, and database all
together, this infrastructure splits each of those components onto
its own dedicated server(s). This is what "scaling up" means here:
each layer of the stack now has its own server(s) that can be scaled,
maintained, and secured independently.

For every additional element, why it is added:

- A second load balancer (HAProxy), configured as a cluster with the
  first one:
  A single load balancer is a SPOF: if it goes down, users cannot
  reach any server, even though the servers behind it are healthy.
  Adding a second HAProxy node, clustered with the first (for example
  using a shared virtual IP with keepalived), removes this single
  point of failure. If the active load balancer fails, the second one
  takes over automatically, and traffic keeps flowing.

- Splitting the web server onto its own server(s):
  Separating the web server (Nginx) from the rest of the stack allows
  it to be scaled independently based on the number of incoming HTTP
  connections, without being limited by the resources needed for the
  application logic or the database. It also isolates it so that a
  problem in the application server or database does not directly
  crash the web server layer.

- Splitting the application server onto its own server(s):
  Separating the application server (which runs the codebase) from
  the web server and database allows this layer to be scaled based
  on how much processing power the business logic needs, independent
  of web traffic handling or database storage needs. It also makes it
  easier to deploy new code (restart/update only the application
  server) without touching the web server or database.

- Splitting the database onto its own dedicated server:
  Separating the database (MySQL) from the web and application
  servers allows the database to have dedicated resources (CPU, RAM,
  disk I/O) tuned specifically for data storage and queries, which
  are very different resource needs from serving web pages or running
  application code. It also isolates the most sensitive/critical data
  layer, making it easier to secure, back up, and scale (for example,
  adding read replicas) separately from the rest of the infrastructure.

Overall, splitting each component onto its own server, combined with
a clustered pair of load balancers, allows each layer of the
infrastructure to be scaled, maintained, monitored, and secured
independently, and removes the load balancer as a single point of
failure, making the whole system more resilient and better prepared
to handle increased traffic.
